From: oveRTOS
Subject: [PATCH] util/main-loop: sleep after an empty poll on Emscripten

Emscripten proxies poll() to the browser thread, which checks readiness
once and returns.  When the guest is idle, QEMU's main loop spins.
After an empty poll with a non-zero timeout, sleep for up to 1 ms.
This caps the added delay for a new fd event or timer at about 1 ms
plus scheduling time.  The main loop has released the BQL and replay
lock here, so g_usleep() can block its pthread on a futex without an
Asyncify unwind.

In the FreeRTOS Linux browser bundle, idle host CPU use fell from 1.74
to 0.72 cores (median of 3 runs).  Over 3 rounds each, key echo p50 was
32-40 ms before and 33-48 ms after, p95 49-50 ms before and 49 ms
after, and the guest's five-second sleep took 5.54-5.72 s before and
5.48-5.54 s after.  A shell loop ran slightly faster (18.4 to 17.2 s).

---
--- a/util/main-loop.c
+++ b/util/main-loop.c
@@ -308,6 +308,18 @@
     replay_mutex_unlock();
 
     ret = qemu_poll_ns((GPollFD *)gpollfds->data, gpollfds->len, timeout);
+#ifdef EMSCRIPTEN
+    /* Emscripten's poll never blocks: wait out part of an empty timeout. */
+    if (ret == 0 && timeout != 0) {
+        int64_t us = 1000;
+        if (timeout > 0 && timeout / 1000 < us) {
+            us = timeout / 1000;
+        }
+        if (us > 0) {
+            g_usleep(us);
+        }
+    }
+#endif
 
     replay_mutex_lock();
     bql_lock();
